<h2>使用建议</h2>
<p>每次周会复盘时，拿出这份清单过一遍。先从「未通过」项最多的素材开始优化，再逐项验证改进效果。</p>
<p>不要试图一次解决所有问题。按优先级排序：先抓「数据不足」和「频次失控」这类结构性问题，再优化文案和设计细节。</p>
<p>素材能跑是基础，能卖才是目标。把检查清单贴在工位旁边，让每个素材过审时都有一个客观的判断依据。</p>
            </div>
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>In Meta advertising, the biggest mistake isn't creatives that fail to generate impressions—it's creatives that get plenty of clicks but zero conversions.</p>
<p><strong>"Can run" creative:</strong> High CTR, low CPM, fast spend, massive reach.<br />
<strong>"Can sell" creative:</strong> Positive ROAS, low CPA, complete conversion path, expected user behavior.</p>
<p>The two don't always correlate. Many optimizers only check CTR and spend during reviews, ignoring the metrics that actually drive sales.</p>
<p>Here's a direct-to-use creative review checklist with clear criteria and action steps for each item.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Check 1: Is CTR from Real Intent?</h2>
<p><strong>How to check:</strong> Compare page scroll rate against CTR. If CTR exceeds 2% but page scroll rate is below 30%, those clicks are likely "accidental taps" rather than intentional interest.</p>
<p><strong>Pass criteria:</strong> At least 50% of clicking users scroll to the product details or core value proposition.</p>
<p><strong>If failed:</strong> Optimize clickbait copy. Check for misleading design elements (arrows pointing to wrong areas, button colors causing confusion).</p>
<hr />
<h2>Check 2: Is There a Funnel Break?</h2>
<p><strong>How to check:</strong> View the sequence: Impressions → Clicks → Add to Cart → Checkout → Purchase. Each step's drop-off shouldn't exceed 1.5x industry benchmark.</p>
<p><strong>Pass criteria:</strong> Add to cart rate / clicks ≥ 15%; Purchase rate / cart adds ≥ 20%.</p>
<p><strong>If failed:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Low cart rate → Slow page load or unclear product info</li>
<li>Low checkout rate → Complex payment flow, check payment method coverage</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Check 3: Is Frequency Causing Fatigue?</h2>
<p><strong>How to check:</strong> Review Frequency data under Campaign → Delivery. When a single user sees the same creative 3+ times in 7 days, CVR drops significantly.</p>
<p><strong>Pass criteria:</strong> Average frequency ≤ 2.5, stable or slightly declining over time.</p>
<p><strong>If failed:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Split audiences to reduce overlap</li>
<li>Enable creative rotation so different variations alternate</li>
<li>Pause high-frequency audiences for 14 days before retargeting</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Check 4: Is Audience Matching Off?</h2>
<p><strong>How to check:</strong> Review Audience Overlap at the ad set level. High overlap (&gt;30%) means the same user is being reached multiple times by different creatives, muddying attribution.</p>
<p><strong>Pass criteria:</strong> Core audience and expanded audience overlap &lt; 25%.</p>
<p><strong>If failed:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Simplify audience structure to avoid the same user appearing in multiple ad sets</li>
<li>Use "AND" combinations of interests + behaviors to reduce broad targeting</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Check 5: Is the Creative in Decline?</h2>
<p><strong>How to check:</strong> Track Cost per Result in 3-day increments. Normal creatives fluctuate within ±15%; declining creatives show Cost per Result rising for 3 consecutive days, exceeding the initial value by 30%.</p>
<p><strong>Pass criteria:</strong> Cost per Result shows no consecutive upward trend.</p>
<p><strong>If failed:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Prepare 2-3 backup creatives to swap in when Cost per Result rises 15%</li>
<li>Test "element tweaks" (new background color, different hook copy) to extend the creative's lifespan</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Check 6: Is Sample Size Sufficient?</h2>
<p><strong>How to check:</strong> Meta recommends each variation accumulate at least 50 conversion events for statistical significance. Below this threshold, mark as "insufficient data."</p>
<p><strong>Pass criteria:</strong> Each creative variation achieves at least 50 conversion events.</p>
<p><strong>If failed:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Don't draw conclusions until Statistical Significance reaches 95%</li>
<li>Extend test duration or increase budget to accelerate data collection</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Check 7: Does Conversion Value Cover Cost?</h2>
<p><strong>How to check:</strong> Calculate expected LTV (Lifetime Value) per converted customer. If CPA exceeds 30% of LTV, you're losing money long-term even if conversion volume looks decent.</p>
<p><strong>Pass criteria:</strong> CPA ≤ Expected LTV × 30%.</p>
<p><strong>If failed:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>For creatives with high CPA but barely positive ROAS, lower bids or tighten audiences</li>
<li>Investigate for fake orders or invalid conversions dragging down data quality</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Check 8: Creative-to-Landing Page Consistency</h2>
<p><strong>How to check:</strong> Visually compare the ad creative with the landing page hero section: main colors, product images, key copy points. Inconsistency creates a "jarring experience" that destroys trust.</p>
<p><strong>Pass criteria:</strong> Users can identify the core value proposition within 3 seconds of arriving at the landing page.</p>
<p><strong>If failed:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Establish a "Creative-to-LP" verification SOP requiring designers to deliver landing page versions alongside ads</li>
<li>Test full-funnel closure using Meta's Instant Experience</li>
</ul>
